## Problem

Assertions (or calls to helper functions that assert internally) placed directly in a `describe()` callback body — instead of inside `test()`/`it()` — fail SILENTLY under Node's built-in test runner on Node v24 when invoked as:

```
node --test --test-force-exit test/*.test.js
```

The runner prints a ✖ for the suite, but the summary reports `fail 0` and the process **exits 0**. CI stays green while a load-bearing structural guard (e.g. "this function must still exist in the source file") has stopped running.

## Reproduction (Node v24.13.0)

```js
const { describe, it } = require('node:test');
const assert = require('node:assert');
describe('outer', () => {
  assert.ok(false, 'runs at collection time');   // ✖ printed, but fail 0 / exit 0
  it('inner', () => { assert.ok(true); });
});
```

Result: `tests 0 / pass 0 / fail 0`, exit code 0.

The same trap fires one hop away: a common test idiom defines a slice helper like `function sliceHandler(marker) { assert.notEqual(src.indexOf(marker), -1); ... }` and calls it at describe scope (`describe('x', () => { const h = sliceHandler(...); ... })`). The helper's assert runs at collection time and is equally silent.

## Verified failure-semantics matrix (same flags)

- assert in `describe()` body: printed ✖, **fail 0, exit 0** — SILENT
- assert in `before()` hook: fail 0 in summary, but **exit 1** — LOUD at the exit-code level (tests show as cancelled)
- assert in `test()`/`it()` body: **fail 1, exit 1** — loud

## Fix

Convert describe-scope computed slices to lazy assignment in a hook:

```js
describe('x', () => {
  let h;
  before(() => { h = sliceHandler('marker'); });  // a missing marker now exits 1
  it('...', () => { assert.ok(h.includes('y')); });
});
```

## Preventing re-entry

A grep-level guard is not enough (regex literals containing braces, `it('x', { timeout: N }, fn)` options-objects, and template literals all break naive brace tracking). A small lexer that skips strings/comments/regex literals, tracks which callback frame encloses each `assert`, treats `before/beforeEach/after/afterEach` as safe frames, and also flags describe-scope CALLS to file-local functions whose bodies assert, can run as a meta-guard test asserting zero findings across the test directory.

## Caveats

- Behavior verified on Node v24.13.0 with `--test-force-exit`; older Node lines may differ (some report suite-level failures in the fail count).
- Module-scope (top-of-file) throws DO fail the file loudly — the silent class is specifically describe-callback scope.
- `before()` failures exit 1 but still show `fail 0` in the summary; if your CI parses the summary text instead of the exit code, prefer moving guards into `test()` bodies.

## Problem

Integration-testing an interactive Node CLI (multiple sequential `readline.question()` prompts on a SHARED `readline.createInterface`) by spawning it as a subprocess and writing all answers to stdin up-front (`child.stdin.write('all\ny\n'); child.stdin.end()`) silently loses every answer after the first.

**Symptom:** the CLI prints prompt 1, consumes answer 1 correctly, prints prompt 2 — then exits with code 0 without ever processing answer 2. No error, no hang under default timeouts: the pending `question()` promise never resolves, stdin has ended, the event loop empties, and Node exits 0 mid-await. From the test's perspective the process "completed" but skipped a whole branch.

## Root cause

`readline.Interface` processes an incoming stdin chunk into lines immediately. `question()` only captures a line if its callback is registered WHEN the line event fires. With `"all\ny\n"` arriving as one chunk: line `"all"` resolves question 1, then line `"y"` is emitted in the same tick — before the code between the two prompts runs and before question 2's callback is registered. A line emitted while no question is pending goes to the interface's generic `'line'` event; with no listener, it is dropped. Then stdin EOF closes the interface, so question 2 can never be answered.

Note: a shared interface (vs. a fresh interface per question) does NOT fix this — sharing fixes a different bug (buffered input lost when the previous interface closes), not inter-question line loss.

## Solution: scripted prompt driver

Write each answer only when its prompt appears on the child's stdout:

```js
function runCli(args, promptScript, { timeoutMs = 30000 } = {}) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const child = spawn(process.execPath, args, { stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'] });
    const script = [...promptScript];   // [{ re: /Which clients\?/, send: 'all\n' }, ...]
    let stdout = '', stderr = '';
    const feed = () => {
      while (script.length > 0 && script[0].re.test(stdout)) {
        child.stdin.write(script.shift().send);
        if (script.length === 0) child.stdin.end(); // no further prompts expected
      }
    };
    child.stdout.on('data', (c) => { stdout += c; feed(); });
    child.stderr.on('data', (c) => { stderr += c; });
    const timer = setTimeout(() => { child.kill('SIGKILL');
      reject(new Error(`hung; unanswered prompts: ${script.length}\n${stdout}\n${stderr}`)); }, timeoutMs);
    child.on('close', (code) => { clearTimeout(timer);
      if (script.length > 0) return reject(new Error(`exited with ${script.length} prompt(s) never shown`));
      resolve({ code, stdout, stderr });
    });
  });
}
```

Rejecting when the process exits with prompts still unanswered is important — it turns the silent-skip failure mode into a loud test failure.

## Caveats

- `question()` prompts are written to the interface's `output` stream, so they DO appear on the subprocess's stdout — matching on them is reliable.
- Keep prompt regexes unique per prompt; match against the CUMULATIVE stdout buffer.
- Ending stdin after the last scripted answer is safe; ending it earlier races the EOF against unanswered questions.
- This also explains why `printf "a\nb\n" | mycli` "works sometimes": if answers arrive in separate chunks (terminal typing, slow pipes) the timing can accidentally line up. Single-chunk pipes reliably lose the later answers.

## Outcome

Verified against a real multi-prompt installer CLI under `node:test`: the up-front pipe reproducibly lost answer 2 (exit 0, branch skipped); the prompt driver answers all prompts deterministically across parallel test runs.

**Problem:** A common Node.js behavioral-test pattern stages the app in a temp dir (copy entry file, symlink `node_modules`), gated by a dependency probe so CI without deps skips instead of failing:

```js
try {
  require.resolve('some-dep', { paths: [REPO_ROOT] });
} catch { t.skip('deps not installed'); return; }
// ...
fs.symlinkSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'node_modules'), path.join(tmpDir, 'node_modules'));
```

This works in a normal checkout but **fails in a git worktree** — and the failure mode is confusing: the gate PASSES but the spawned app dies with `Cannot find module 'some-dep'` (MODULE_NOT_FOUND), so the test hard-fails instead of skipping, and only in worktrees. Teams misdiagnose it as "environment-only" flakiness.

**Root cause:** two different resolution behaviors. `require.resolve(dep, { paths: [dir] })` does NOT stop at `dir/node_modules` — it walks every ancestor directory's `node_modules` too. A worktree at `/.claude/worktrees/` (or any nested worktree path) has no local `node_modules`, but resolution walks up and finds the MAIN checkout's install, so the probe succeeds. The staging step, however, symlinks the literal path `REPO_ROOT/node_modules` — which doesn't exist in the worktree — so either no link is created (if guarded by `fs.existsSync`) or a dangling link is staged. The temp dir (usually under `os.tmpdir()`) has no ancestor with a `node_modules`, so the spawned child resolves nothing.

**Fix:** derive the real `node_modules` directory from the module the probe actually resolved, and symlink THAT:

```js
let nodeModulesDir;
try {
  const entry = require.resolve('some-dep', { paths: [REPO_ROOT] });
  nodeModulesDir = entry.slice(0, entry.lastIndexOf(`${path.sep}node_modules${path.sep}`) + '/node_modules'.length);
} catch { t.skip('deps not installed'); return; }
// ...
fs.symlinkSync(nodeModulesDir, path.join(tmpDir, 'node_modules'));
```

This is correct in the main checkout (resolves to its own `node_modules`), in worktrees (resolves to the main checkout's), and still skips cleanly when deps are genuinely absent.

**Caveats:**
- Slicing at the LAST `node_modules` path segment matters — the entry path can contain nested `node_modules` for transitive deps.
- If the dep is pnpm-installed, the resolved entry lives under `node_modules/.pnpm/...`; the last-segment slice still lands on a usable dir for symlinking because the virtual store sits inside it, but verify on pnpm repos.
- Verified on Node 24 (`node --test`, macOS); the resolution semantics are longstanding CommonJS behavior, so this applies broadly.
